1
00:00:08,782 --> 00:00:32,187
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought of doing some proper vipassana, as in the interrogation, self-interrogation, that obviously, as we spoke before, results... can result, if done rightly, in establishments of the mind, in samādhi.

2
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:43,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the proper vipassana of self-interrogation would bring forward samādhi, kompoccha, upon the right themes, wholesome themes, themes free from greed, aversion and delusion.

3
00:00:48,423 --> 00:00:51,484
[SPEAKER_00]: I think previously, the one we've done before, it was kind of about suffering, wasn't it?

4
00:00:51,564 --> 00:00:52,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling and suffering.

5
00:00:52,364 --> 00:00:53,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling, yeah.

6
00:00:53,884 --> 00:00:55,024
[SPEAKER_00]: But I thought, eh...

7
00:00:57,425 --> 00:01:02,546
[SPEAKER_00]: I know we can do the one in terms of... well, in terms of the unwholesome, in terms of sensuality or in terms of ill will.

8
00:01:05,497 --> 00:01:13,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, like, it can be done by anyone, to oneself.

9
00:01:17,665 --> 00:01:19,066
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, right here, right now.

10
00:01:23,749 --> 00:01:31,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there... is there sensuality present in the background of your mind, in any shape or form?

11
00:01:34,297 --> 00:01:45,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there the desire, or the desire for the wanting of some pleasure based upon the senses?

12
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:45,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

13
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:46,460
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, I can...

14
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:47,500
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not just asking you.

15
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:51,421
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm putting the question out and I'll also describe whatever.

16
00:01:53,022 --> 00:01:54,282
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, yeah, you can say that.

17
00:01:54,402 --> 00:02:00,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a desire for wanting a pleasure on a kind of sense basis?

18
00:02:01,344 --> 00:02:02,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what saṃsāra is.

19
00:02:04,257 --> 00:02:09,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I entertain a possibility that somehow... Well, no.

20
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:09,920
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the further.

21
00:02:10,341 --> 00:02:10,481
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

22
00:02:10,521 --> 00:02:17,285
[SPEAKER_00]: For now, is there... is there a desire for pleasure of the senses?

23
00:02:23,409 --> 00:02:23,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes or no?

24
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:26,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Just the most generally.

25
00:02:28,512 --> 00:02:30,713
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to look for every little what-if.

26
00:02:30,853 --> 00:02:31,033
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

27
00:02:31,053 --> 00:02:33,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Just overall, is my mind now

28
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:41,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Under the pressure of sensuality, or is my mind now not under the pressure of sensuality?

29
00:02:43,012 --> 00:02:45,013
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the question a person should be asking oneself.

30
00:02:45,313 --> 00:02:48,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, right here, right now.

31
00:02:48,874 --> 00:02:54,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, because I'm sitting in a comfortable position.

32
00:02:54,235 --> 00:02:54,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

33
00:02:54,555 --> 00:02:56,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining it.

34
00:02:56,776 --> 00:02:57,716
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not sensuality.

35
00:02:57,856 --> 00:02:59,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but I'm trying to maintain that.

36
00:02:59,597 --> 00:03:00,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever there's a

37
00:03:01,897 --> 00:03:05,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but avoiding this comfort is not sensual.

38
00:03:05,438 --> 00:03:06,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily sensual.

39
00:03:07,078 --> 00:03:08,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to maintain the comfort.

40
00:03:08,939 --> 00:03:09,139
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

41
00:03:09,319 --> 00:03:18,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure of sensuality is basically pleasure that you can gain through the sense bases engaging with its sensual counterparts.

42
00:03:20,241 --> 00:03:20,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust.

43
00:03:21,622 --> 00:03:21,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Greed.

44
00:03:23,182 --> 00:03:25,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you lustful about sitting in a comfortable chair?

45
00:03:26,503 --> 00:03:26,723
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

46
00:03:27,296 --> 00:03:28,816
[SPEAKER_01]: You have an option, you are... you're sitting.

47
00:03:28,956 --> 00:03:30,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it gets uncomfortable in this position.

48
00:03:31,377 --> 00:03:37,578
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not like... that's when it's like... you have to start differentiating the type of pleasure that is unwholesome.

49
00:03:38,318 --> 00:03:40,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people can't... can't see that.

50
00:03:40,858 --> 00:03:47,380
[SPEAKER_00]: So often people will, like, assume you must forsake everything, everything, every little desire.

51
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:47,680
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

52
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:49,480
[SPEAKER_00]: There are desires that are not desires.

53
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:54,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoiding discomfort is just intentional for an arahant as well.

54
00:03:55,896 --> 00:03:59,438
[SPEAKER_00]: But I mean lust, aversion, delusion, sensuality.

55
00:04:00,098 --> 00:04:17,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any, as the Buddha would say, like, desire in regard to the world, or sort of displeasure in regard to the world, which is rooted on the same domain, discontent, craving for...

56
00:04:17,207 --> 00:04:21,930
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the question a proper vipassana can start with.

57
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:24,151
[SPEAKER_00]: In my mind, right here, right now,

58
00:04:25,443 --> 00:04:33,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Under the pressure of sensual craving, of wanting, the wanting of pleasure, pleasure of the senses.

59
00:04:35,407 --> 00:04:37,088
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the answer will have to be yes or no.

60
00:04:40,369 --> 00:04:42,610
[SPEAKER_00]: On that general level.

61
00:04:42,670 --> 00:04:46,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my mind currently affected with sensuality, as Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta would say?

62
00:04:47,792 --> 00:04:49,153
[SPEAKER_01]: As far as I can tell, no.

63
00:04:49,353 --> 00:04:49,533
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

64
00:04:49,853 --> 00:04:50,393
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's fine.

65
00:04:50,773 --> 00:04:54,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the answer, like, let's start, let's say the answer is no.

66
00:04:55,383 --> 00:04:57,144
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I'm not completely sure.

67
00:04:57,704 --> 00:04:59,845
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how probably many people would answer it.

68
00:05:01,225 --> 00:05:03,786
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no, but I'm not completely sure.

69
00:05:04,226 --> 00:05:05,967
[SPEAKER_00]: What does not being completely sure mean?

70
00:05:07,207 --> 00:05:09,788
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you're not free from doubt in regard to it.

71
00:05:12,109 --> 00:05:18,971
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're not free from doubt in regard to a dhamma, a phenomena, you can't say you're free from it.

72
00:05:19,791 --> 00:05:21,752
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't say you're free from that domain.

73
00:05:22,704 --> 00:05:27,386
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where freedom from doubt, for a Sotāpanna, is like the first step towards the right view.

74
00:05:27,826 --> 00:05:37,549
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the same principle, then, that he can develop... Like, Sotāpanna is not free from sensuality, for example, because he's still not free from doubt in regard to sensual domain.

75
00:05:37,949 --> 00:05:43,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas the Buddha would say he still hasn't fully understood the gratification, the danger and the escape.

76
00:05:43,351 --> 00:05:47,352
[SPEAKER_00]: You understand that fully means you fully went beyond doubt in regard to the nature of sensuality.

77
00:05:49,890 --> 00:05:51,031
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a fine starting point.

78
00:05:51,051 --> 00:05:59,641
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can say, no, currently, my mind is not under the influence of sensuality, but I'm not completely sure about it.

79
00:06:00,822 --> 00:06:05,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, does that mean that there might be a way that sensuality is influencing you?

80
00:06:08,371 --> 00:06:08,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe.

81
00:06:09,312 --> 00:06:12,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean there might be a way that sensuality might influence me?

82
00:06:13,093 --> 00:06:14,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's more like it.

83
00:06:14,754 --> 00:06:15,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

84
00:06:15,515 --> 00:06:30,242
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically, you recognize that currently, the mind is not under the pressure of sensuality, but you also simultaneously there, but simply by saying, hmm, I'm not sure, means the possibility of sensuality, you recognize you are not free.

85
00:06:30,823 --> 00:06:30,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

86
00:06:31,123 --> 00:06:31,683
[SPEAKER_00]: There could be.

87
00:06:31,963 --> 00:06:32,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

88
00:06:32,784 --> 00:06:33,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I know what it is.

89
00:06:33,944 --> 00:06:35,045
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point here.

90
00:06:35,485 --> 00:06:41,147
[SPEAKER_00]: To start discerning the possibility of sensuality, that you are still liable to.

91
00:06:42,748 --> 00:06:48,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But that need... like, you can't wait for it to be manifest in the future, to deal with it.

92
00:06:48,691 --> 00:06:52,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility, it has to be dealt as possibility.

93
00:06:52,572 --> 00:06:57,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, if in the future the sensuality manifests, that's not the possibility now that you're thinking of.

94
00:06:57,454 --> 00:07:03,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, it came through the domain of possibility, but at that time, in the future, you're gonna... another domain of possibility in regard to it.

95
00:07:04,488 --> 00:07:08,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Because possibility is a part of that structural domain of manifested things.

96
00:07:09,430 --> 00:07:11,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that are present and its possibilities.

97
00:07:11,892 --> 00:07:12,412
[SPEAKER_00]: It's implicated.

98
00:07:12,452 --> 00:07:14,013
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what gives things the meaning.

99
00:07:17,814 --> 00:07:18,415
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

100
00:07:18,595 --> 00:07:27,699
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility of sensuality that you implied through the answer, that one will imply if they say, no, currently I'm not...

101
00:07:27,719 --> 00:07:28,940
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not oppressed by sensuality.

102
00:07:30,006 --> 00:07:30,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I'm not quite sure.

103
00:07:30,927 --> 00:07:34,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, okay, the time will come when I will be oppressed by sensuality.

104
00:07:35,231 --> 00:07:38,914
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that also means that the reason that I'm not oppressed now is not fully understood.

105
00:07:39,034 --> 00:07:39,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

106
00:07:40,075 --> 00:07:41,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I not oppressed now?

107
00:07:41,936 --> 00:07:44,138
[SPEAKER_00]: And why I might be oppressed in the future?

108
00:07:44,839 --> 00:07:47,121
[SPEAKER_00]: With the next one that comes.

109
00:07:47,862 --> 00:07:50,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding that possibility of oppression.

110
00:07:50,839 --> 00:07:51,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

111
00:07:51,199 --> 00:07:56,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Needs to be done in the present, presently arisen experience that now you're having vipassana about.

112
00:07:56,621 --> 00:07:58,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you overcome the whole domain.

113
00:07:58,902 --> 00:08:07,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the root of the domain, central domain, domain of anger, domain of unwholesome, is not in the particular manifestation of those.

114
00:08:07,565 --> 00:08:10,806
[SPEAKER_00]: The root is you being liable to the possibility of those.

115
00:08:10,826 --> 00:08:11,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with suffering.

116
00:08:12,047 --> 00:08:12,947
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why dukkha,

117
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:21,084
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like... not necessarily the tremendous, horrific torture, pain and discomfort.

118
00:08:22,165 --> 00:08:23,186
[SPEAKER_00]: It's this is.

119
00:08:23,486 --> 00:08:25,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha is opposite of sukha.

120
00:08:25,847 --> 00:08:29,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, would be translated as this is, unease.

121
00:08:30,870 --> 00:08:32,991
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's the nature of human experience.

122
00:08:33,051 --> 00:08:33,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha.

123
00:08:33,472 --> 00:08:35,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that everybody suffers all the time.

124
00:08:36,113 --> 00:08:36,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at all.

125
00:08:37,954 --> 00:08:42,537
[SPEAKER_00]: But possibility in the... all the time in human experience is

126
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:45,030
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that of uneasiness.

127
00:08:45,931 --> 00:08:46,652
[SPEAKER_00]: That is implied.

128
00:08:47,032 --> 00:08:47,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

129
00:08:47,392 --> 00:08:51,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Because things get, at any given moment, in the future.

130
00:08:52,917 --> 00:08:59,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a present possibility of things in the future, at any given moment, going not the way you want them to.

131
00:08:59,943 --> 00:09:07,369
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said, fundamentally, suffering is basically being separated from things you want, being united with things you don't want.

132
00:09:08,430 --> 00:09:10,731
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, united with feelings and perceptions you don't want.

133
00:09:11,252 --> 00:09:12,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Separated from the ones you want.

134
00:09:13,576 --> 00:09:18,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, just misconceiving the five aggregates, misconceiving the experience as a whole, that's why you suffer.

135
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:22,422
[SPEAKER_00]: And you misconceive it as you're the one in control, you're the one in charge.

136
00:09:23,883 --> 00:09:29,927
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, every next second, when another thing manifests, it can prove you wrong.

137
00:09:30,567 --> 00:09:34,550
[SPEAKER_00]: And you remain liable to that for as long as you maintain assumption that you're in charge.

138
00:09:37,733 --> 00:09:46,577
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you stop at any assumption you're in charge, you have surmounted the domain of possibility of being proven wrong, that you are not in control, on account of which you would suffer.

139
00:09:49,518 --> 00:09:54,160
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

140
00:09:54,181 --> 00:10:01,604
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibilities must be understood as possibilities within the presently enduring experience as a whole.

141
00:10:03,032 --> 00:10:09,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like momentary glimpses of figuring out or discerning why and how and working some methodical patterns.

142
00:10:09,737 --> 00:10:10,177
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that.

143
00:10:11,618 --> 00:10:18,343
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a simple question and a simple answer, but you need to know what to look for it and then you get to see the signs of things that matter.

144
00:10:19,904 --> 00:10:21,785
[SPEAKER_00]: And is my mind currently pressured with sensuality?

145
00:10:22,426 --> 00:10:22,646
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

146
00:10:23,867 --> 00:10:26,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Can my mind be then pressured with sensuality?

147
00:10:27,269 --> 00:10:28,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Hmm, maybe.

148
00:10:28,850 --> 00:10:29,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe means yes.

149
00:10:30,231 --> 00:10:31,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Doubt means yes.

150
00:10:32,586 --> 00:10:37,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so my mind can be pressured by sensuality.

151
00:10:37,087 --> 00:10:43,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have to wait for that in the future to prove that yes, truly, it was pressured?

152
00:10:44,388 --> 00:10:46,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I already know that simply by doubting it?

153
00:10:48,229 --> 00:10:48,969
[SPEAKER_00]: You already know it.

154
00:10:49,709 --> 00:10:55,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't understand the nature of that possible pressure, you're liable to it.

155
00:10:56,610 --> 00:10:58,170
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's your starting point, by the way.

156
00:10:58,971 --> 00:11:01,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Starting point is you're liable to that pressure.

157
00:11:02,496 --> 00:11:08,019
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like a perfect satipaṭṭhāna, and then, occasionally, Puthujjana makes mistakes, and then, steps back into sensuality.

158
00:11:08,099 --> 00:11:08,259
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

159
00:11:08,679 --> 00:11:17,904
[SPEAKER_00]: He's Puthujjana, not free from sensuality, because he hasn't understood that domain of possible pressure of sensuality, possible liability.

160
00:11:19,505 --> 00:11:22,847
[SPEAKER_00]: If liability is possible, means, overall, you are liable.

161
00:11:23,728 --> 00:11:28,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Not always, but sometimes, and sometimes, it's enough for the nature of sensuality to still pertain to you.

162
00:11:30,495 --> 00:11:43,301
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't want nature of sensuality to still pertain to you, or nature of doubt, or nature of suffering, or nature of anger, or nature of anything unwholesome, that possibility of pertaining to you needs to be understood and abandoned right here, right now.

163
00:11:44,502 --> 00:11:45,442
[SPEAKER_00]: As possibility.

164
00:11:45,943 --> 00:11:46,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Needs to be discerned.

165
00:11:47,403 --> 00:11:55,167
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we always talk about the peripheral awareness, the mindfulness of, like, the signs, because it's on the level of signs and tokens of the presently manifested things.

166
00:11:56,308 --> 00:11:56,548
[SPEAKER_00]: As in,

167
00:11:57,272 --> 00:12:07,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Currently, presently, mind is not oppressed by sensuality, but it signs its tokens, its significances, that it might be in the future.

168
00:12:07,798 --> 00:12:08,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what binds you.

169
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:18,824
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that the mind is currently pressured or not pressured, that's basically a result of your liability to that domain in the first place.

170
00:12:20,806 --> 00:12:24,808
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you don't need to wait for it to be pressured to know that you are pressured.

171
00:12:27,411 --> 00:12:34,857
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to wait for it to realize that you're not in control, like, to be forced upon you through, like, accidents or whatever things happening.

172
00:12:35,638 --> 00:12:38,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Just the fact that it might happen means you're not in control.

173
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:49,569
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you don't need to have anything horrible happen to you in order to become aware of the dukkha, the fundamental sign of human experience.

174
00:12:49,669 --> 00:12:52,291
[SPEAKER_00]: But it won't be seen on the particular basis.

175
00:12:52,671 --> 00:12:55,093
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a symptom of your liability.

176
00:12:59,288 --> 00:13:01,549
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's get back to the presently...

177
00:13:01,569 --> 00:13:02,789
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a possibility.

178
00:13:03,350 --> 00:13:04,810
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a possibility, exactly.

179
00:13:05,150 --> 00:13:05,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Which...

180
00:13:06,231 --> 00:13:07,311
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's it.

181
00:13:07,411 --> 00:13:16,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now people would generally, like... What the mind would usually do, is try to rationalize it.

182
00:13:17,015 --> 00:13:24,838
[SPEAKER_00]: But finding out why or how, because of this or because of that, it's all the result of your liability in the first place.

183
00:13:26,023 --> 00:13:31,367
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha meant, hit by the first arrow.

184
00:13:32,307 --> 00:13:32,948
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't see that.

185
00:13:33,008 --> 00:13:35,169
[SPEAKER_00]: He's too concerned about figuring out who hit him.

186
00:13:35,529 --> 00:13:37,751
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the name of the client from the man who shot him?

187
00:13:37,771 --> 00:13:38,371
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the name of this?

188
00:13:38,591 --> 00:13:39,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is the surgeon?

189
00:13:39,372 --> 00:13:39,832
[SPEAKER_00]: What are they?

190
00:13:40,272 --> 00:13:40,432
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

191
00:13:40,553 --> 00:13:41,413
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all irrelevant.

192
00:13:41,713 --> 00:13:43,815
[SPEAKER_00]: What is relevant is that you were touched in the first place.

193
00:13:44,895 --> 00:13:45,996
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you're liable to it.

194
00:13:46,556 --> 00:13:51,239
[SPEAKER_00]: So, possibility of sensuality is something that the mind is liable to.

195
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:55,062
[SPEAKER_00]: In this presently enduring experience as a whole.

196
00:14:07,868 --> 00:14:10,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can let that endure or sink in.

197
00:14:11,430 --> 00:14:12,891
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to figure it out.

198
00:14:15,853 --> 00:14:21,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Start recognizing the phenomenon of enduring possibility as a phenomenon of enduring possibility.

199
00:14:28,241 --> 00:14:29,322
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your attitude then?

200
00:14:31,684 --> 00:14:35,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards possibility of sensuality.

201
00:14:38,961 --> 00:14:40,301
[SPEAKER_01]: I immediately think I want to be careful.

202
00:14:41,402 --> 00:14:42,602
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be careful, okay.

203
00:14:43,802 --> 00:14:44,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you want to be careful?

204
00:14:45,883 --> 00:14:51,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I want... because I want that possibility to become an actuality.

205
00:14:51,965 --> 00:14:53,205
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to be affected by it.

206
00:14:53,245 --> 00:14:54,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

207
00:14:55,026 --> 00:14:56,006
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you already are.

208
00:14:56,046 --> 00:14:56,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

209
00:14:58,547 --> 00:14:59,227
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all fine.

210
00:14:59,327 --> 00:15:01,588
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no, like, right or wrong answer.

211
00:15:03,387 --> 00:15:16,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Through doing this kind of interrogation, you get to clarify and see signs and tokens of presently enduring phenomena, presently enduring dhamma, and then the mind starts to discern them, as also presently enduring dhamma on a more peripheral level.

212
00:15:17,690 --> 00:15:22,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how the mind gets established upon the right things, upon non-sensuality, non-greed, non-aversion.

213
00:15:23,491 --> 00:15:29,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself then, or whoever listens to this can ask oneself then, why

214
00:15:32,572 --> 00:15:40,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I be affected by the possibility of arising, of sensuality arising?

215
00:15:40,959 --> 00:15:43,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would... Would I be affected?

216
00:15:44,221 --> 00:15:44,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe.

217
00:15:45,262 --> 00:15:45,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

218
00:15:45,622 --> 00:15:49,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would I clarify that in the present?

219
00:15:49,605 --> 00:15:52,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like waiting for the thing, but on the level of possibility.

220
00:15:52,828 --> 00:15:57,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's assume that you would be affected, because in the past you always were affected.

221
00:15:58,112 --> 00:16:00,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you ask yourself, why am I affected?

222
00:16:03,208 --> 00:16:13,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Why the possibility, right now, right here, right now, possibility of enduring, possibility of arising, of sensuality arising, why am I liable to it, right here, right now?

223
00:16:16,890 --> 00:16:18,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's a possibility.

224
00:16:19,351 --> 00:16:21,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yes, but that's clearly not on you.

225
00:16:21,791 --> 00:16:23,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You did not create that possibility.

226
00:16:23,811 --> 00:16:26,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, what are the characteristics of that possibility?

227
00:16:26,152 --> 00:16:28,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's clarify that angle first.

228
00:16:29,032 --> 00:16:32,913
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the characteristics of the possibility of sensuality, presently enduring?

229
00:16:34,268 --> 00:16:35,749
[SPEAKER_01]: They are characteristics.

230
00:16:36,790 --> 00:16:38,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe it, just in any manner.

231
00:16:38,732 --> 00:16:42,235
[SPEAKER_01]: It's bug... it's connected to the body, isn't it?

232
00:16:42,295 --> 00:16:43,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely, inseparable from the body.

233
00:16:43,696 --> 00:16:44,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Body characteristics.

234
00:16:44,877 --> 00:16:45,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

235
00:16:45,558 --> 00:16:48,881
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something is inseparable from the randomly arisen body, that you have no control over.

236
00:16:48,901 --> 00:16:53,725
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no control over your beating heart, over the operating eyes, nose, smell, taste, touch, and so on.

237
00:16:54,626 --> 00:16:59,911
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is inseparable from it, which means possibility of the sense... sense bases of your body.

238
00:16:59,951 --> 00:17:00,932
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what sensuality is.

239
00:17:01,818 --> 00:17:04,659
[SPEAKER_00]: But that also implies it's kind of structural as well.

240
00:17:05,079 --> 00:17:08,180
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, I wants to go and feed its own domain.

241
00:17:08,220 --> 00:17:08,880
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it does.

242
00:17:09,020 --> 00:17:10,060
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a wild animal.

243
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:18,543
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the five wild animals, their feeding grounds and the possibilities of what they could eat, that's just how things are.

244
00:17:19,823 --> 00:17:20,843
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't created that.

245
00:17:22,244 --> 00:17:22,704
[SPEAKER_00]: The body.

246
00:17:22,964 --> 00:17:24,484
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's important thing to discern.

247
00:17:25,224 --> 00:17:25,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

248
00:17:26,045 --> 00:17:26,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people might

249
00:17:27,863 --> 00:17:34,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Think it's on them, and then try to prevent this particular thing to be, or that, or interfere with this side to... No.

250
00:17:35,187 --> 00:17:37,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, of course, restraint needs to be beforehand.

251
00:17:37,349 --> 00:17:42,592
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to restrain the animals, in order to understand the nature of the feeding ground, and how it affects you.

252
00:17:44,373 --> 00:17:49,376
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the possibility of eye feeding on sights, is the eye's possibility.

253
00:17:51,077 --> 00:17:56,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Smells, possibility of smells is the nose possibility, possibility of touch, bodily possibility.

254
00:17:57,428 --> 00:17:59,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why does that possibility then affect you?

255
00:18:01,669 --> 00:18:06,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Only if it's... if I think it is my possibility.

256
00:18:07,010 --> 00:18:07,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

257
00:18:08,230 --> 00:18:09,130
[SPEAKER_00]: If you appropriate it.

258
00:18:09,750 --> 00:18:10,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you then appropriate it?

259
00:18:11,931 --> 00:18:15,931
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the necessary condition for appropriation that the Buddha said?

260
00:18:17,072 --> 00:18:20,592
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the necessary condition for regarding something as yours?

261
00:18:22,693 --> 00:18:25,233
[SPEAKER_01]: But... for me, regarding...

262
00:18:26,514 --> 00:18:28,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, but why would you regard it as for you?

263
00:18:28,235 --> 00:18:29,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you accept that?

264
00:18:29,236 --> 00:18:30,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay, it is for me.

265
00:18:33,139 --> 00:18:39,944
[SPEAKER_00]: If I come with a gun and a bullet and say, this is for you, would you say, oh, yeah, sure, give it to me, shoot me?

266
00:18:40,804 --> 00:18:41,245
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't.

267
00:18:41,325 --> 00:18:42,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

268
00:18:42,165 --> 00:18:42,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

269
00:18:43,126 --> 00:18:43,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's unpleasant.

270
00:18:44,667 --> 00:18:45,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the pressure of it.

271
00:18:46,028 --> 00:18:50,891
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever there is appropriation, there is welcoming beforehand.

272
00:18:51,112 --> 00:18:52,192
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the necessary basis.

273
00:18:52,653 --> 00:18:55,715
[SPEAKER_00]: There is perceived pleasurability of it.

274
00:18:57,055 --> 00:19:03,578
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sense of self, as Yanagira pointed out, is always developed on the basis of pleasure.

275
00:19:03,998 --> 00:19:08,199
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha says, sabhe saṅkhara anicca, sabhe saṅkhara dukkha.

276
00:19:09,380 --> 00:19:19,044
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see that which is inseparable from this thing that you are taking as yours, as actually unpleasurable, as not wanted.

277
00:19:20,124 --> 00:19:21,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, absolutely true.

278
00:19:21,725 --> 00:19:24,866
[SPEAKER_00]: You are affected by the possibilities of sensuality,

279
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:27,401
[SPEAKER_00]: that actually pertain to your senses, not to you.

280
00:19:28,442 --> 00:19:29,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibilities are not a problem.

281
00:19:30,062 --> 00:19:31,123
[SPEAKER_00]: They are possibilities.

282
00:19:31,923 --> 00:19:35,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Problem is when you start engaging on account of the possibilities, on account of the pressure of possibilities.

283
00:19:37,006 --> 00:19:41,328
[SPEAKER_00]: But you cannot start engaging with them unless you've taken them as pertaining to you.

284
00:19:42,208 --> 00:19:51,913
[SPEAKER_00]: And the necessary basis for taking anything as pertaining to you is regarding it as dear, pleasurable, agreeable, non-threatening.

285
00:19:52,695 --> 00:19:56,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the Buddha's insistence constantly talking about seeing the danger.

286
00:19:56,376 --> 00:19:57,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how you uproot.

287
00:19:57,837 --> 00:20:00,778
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just say, okay, this is not for me anymore.

288
00:20:01,758 --> 00:20:08,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if still there is the implication of pleasurability of it, the assumption for me is automatic.

289
00:20:09,581 --> 00:20:13,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So you undo the assumption for me, not by saying, okay, it's not for me.

290
00:20:14,042 --> 00:20:14,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Good luck with that.

291
00:20:15,223 --> 00:20:19,364
[SPEAKER_00]: But by actually seeing it as unpleasurable, more fundamentally.

292
00:20:20,738 --> 00:20:23,019
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, automatically, it becomes, oh, not for me.

293
00:20:23,480 --> 00:20:25,040
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the simile of the charcoal pit.

294
00:20:25,521 --> 00:20:37,828
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha said, if the two men, two strong men, would come and grab you by the arms and drag you towards... and drop you in a... like a hole dug up full of embers, wouldn't your mind want to be everywhere else except there?

295
00:20:37,848 --> 00:20:41,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention will be turning everywhere else except wanting to go into that hole.

296
00:20:41,850 --> 00:20:46,213
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start regarding the possibility of sensual pleasure like that,

297
00:20:47,724 --> 00:20:50,145
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you stop being liable to the entire domain.

298
00:20:50,625 --> 00:21:00,889
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how whatever present, like particularly, actually arises for your eye, nose, smell, taste, touch, and so on, it will not induce the sensual domain upon you.

299
00:21:02,109 --> 00:21:12,953
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said the Tāgata still sees, hears, recognizes beautiful things in the world, but he has no desire and lust in regard to them, because he freed himself from the assumption of sensual domain.

300
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:15,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Assumption of my essential domain.

301
00:21:16,402 --> 00:21:19,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Assumption of pleasurability of my essential domain.

302
00:21:22,347 --> 00:21:24,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it's just agreeable side, disagreeable side.

303
00:21:25,309 --> 00:21:26,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Agreeable, comfortable chair.

304
00:21:26,931 --> 00:21:28,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Disagreeable, discomfortable chair.

305
00:21:29,413 --> 00:21:30,754
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that is sensuality.

306
00:21:30,854 --> 00:21:31,935
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that will be anger.

307
00:21:32,275 --> 00:21:33,376
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that will be suffering.

308
00:21:33,656 --> 00:21:34,837
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that will be a problem.

309
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:39,161
[SPEAKER_00]: And all, like, all this

310
00:21:40,053 --> 00:21:44,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Just on a basis of couple of right questions and right attitude in regard to it.

311
00:21:45,438 --> 00:21:47,580
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what vipassana truly is.

312
00:21:49,101 --> 00:22:00,771
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we would say, he investigates the meaning, like, to himself, questions himself, or if he sees somebody who might know more, understand more, he goes and gets himself interrogated, basically.

313
00:22:01,951 --> 00:22:11,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how you unravel... Yeah, you know these things to some extent, but you really want to discern them on the basis of the present enduring mindfulness of the experience as a whole.

314
00:22:12,253 --> 00:22:15,574
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, all while we talked, nothing has... the basis hasn't changed.

315
00:22:17,034 --> 00:22:19,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is presently not pressured by any sensuality.

316
00:22:20,755 --> 00:22:23,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but how about possibility of it?

317
00:22:23,535 --> 00:22:28,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I fully certain, free from the extent of sensual domain?

318
00:22:29,477 --> 00:22:29,897
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

319
00:22:30,057 --> 00:22:31,017
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your starting point.

320
00:22:31,708 --> 00:22:32,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know means you're not.

321
00:22:34,731 --> 00:22:35,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Why aren't you?

322
00:22:36,232 --> 00:22:38,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't pertain to you, as you just clarified it.

323
00:22:39,214 --> 00:22:40,996
[SPEAKER_00]: It pertains to the senses.

324
00:22:41,036 --> 00:22:45,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because I'm still actually regarding it as dear and agreeable.

325
00:22:46,220 --> 00:23:00,772
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said you should regard your senses and the mind, I think, in itself as well, like the assassins, like the best friend that, you know, comes and gets your trust and then just wants to kill you at night.

326
00:23:03,939 --> 00:23:05,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like you go paranoid all the time.

327
00:23:05,740 --> 00:23:06,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Just recognize.

328
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:11,602
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I just need to stop regarding this possibility of sensuality as not my friend.

329
00:23:13,083 --> 00:23:19,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Dangerous, perilous, charcoal pit, burning embers, skin disease, all the similarities you're going to get.

330
00:23:20,166 --> 00:23:21,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Not everything all the time.

331
00:23:22,207 --> 00:23:23,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Miserable, ugh, hate.

332
00:23:23,628 --> 00:23:23,828
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

333
00:23:24,548 --> 00:23:30,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Just that degree where you're liable to it, and the possibilities where you're liable to all of it, because of it.

334
00:23:31,594 --> 00:23:39,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stop welcoming that possibility that pertains to the senses, not to you, then that possibility stops bothering you.

335
00:23:40,358 --> 00:23:48,381
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are not liable to the possibility of sensuality, can you then be liable to anything that manifests itself through that possibility of sensuality?

336
00:23:49,541 --> 00:23:51,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely no.

337
00:23:51,622 --> 00:23:59,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever possibility becomes more possible, like here and now, there might be whatever possible

338
00:24:03,893 --> 00:24:07,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're not thinking about the particular, or future, or this might be possible.

339
00:24:07,354 --> 00:24:09,094
[SPEAKER_00]: You think about possibility in its nature.

340
00:24:09,594 --> 00:24:09,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

341
00:24:09,934 --> 00:24:12,895
[SPEAKER_00]: As if, currently, I'm not pressured by any sensual cravings.

342
00:24:14,355 --> 00:24:16,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I say that would be the same for the future?

343
00:24:17,816 --> 00:24:18,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Means...

344
00:24:18,856 --> 00:24:21,116
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there is a sensual craving that comes.

345
00:24:21,877 --> 00:24:22,637
[SPEAKER_00]: As a possibility?

346
00:24:22,897 --> 00:24:23,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as a possibility.

347
00:24:24,417 --> 00:24:25,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it won't be a sensual craving.

348
00:24:26,357 --> 00:24:26,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

349
00:24:26,557 --> 00:24:29,058
[SPEAKER_01]: If you free yourself from the possibility of sensual craving.

350
00:24:29,977 --> 00:24:32,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Like any particular manifestation of sensuality.

351
00:24:32,638 --> 00:24:38,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, the answer is, I look presently oppressed by sensual craving.

352
00:24:38,439 --> 00:24:39,179
[SPEAKER_00]: The answer is yes.

353
00:24:40,099 --> 00:24:43,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, my mind is just thinking about this sensual object.

354
00:24:44,440 --> 00:24:49,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, based on that, okay, so can you then say that in the future you'll be free from sensuality?

355
00:24:49,121 --> 00:24:51,421
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because even now you're not free.

356
00:24:52,302 --> 00:24:53,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first step, what?

357
00:24:53,662 --> 00:24:54,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Restrain.

358
00:24:54,542 --> 00:24:55,882
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't go for a sensual object.

359
00:24:56,182 --> 00:24:59,003
[SPEAKER_00]: But, now, the pressure of it has been revealed.

360
00:25:00,025 --> 00:25:01,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, same questioning.

361
00:25:01,625 --> 00:25:08,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Why, instead of, why would I be pressured in the future, just start with, why am I pressured now?

362
00:25:08,086 --> 00:25:08,347
[SPEAKER_01]: It's...

363
00:25:08,367 --> 00:25:11,667
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a possibility here and now for engaging here and now.

364
00:25:11,967 --> 00:25:13,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibility to engage now.

365
00:25:13,948 --> 00:25:19,389
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm obsessed with cake, so, and there's a cake right in front of me.

366
00:25:19,769 --> 00:25:20,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

367
00:25:20,189 --> 00:25:25,110
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yesterday, I said, oh, there's a possibility that if I see a cake in the future, I will be pressured to go for it.

368
00:25:25,250 --> 00:25:25,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

369
00:25:25,470 --> 00:25:27,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, today, there is a cake in front of me.

370
00:25:27,697 --> 00:25:28,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but that's the thing.

371
00:25:28,917 --> 00:25:32,658
[SPEAKER_01]: But I've... now, the possibility for engaging with that cake is still there.

372
00:25:33,179 --> 00:25:35,359
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I've surmounted that possibility...

373
00:25:35,959 --> 00:25:36,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Yesterday.

374
00:25:36,620 --> 00:25:41,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Yesterday, I'm still surmounting it today, even if the cake is presented.

375
00:25:41,221 --> 00:25:41,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, absolutely.

376
00:25:42,121 --> 00:25:42,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

377
00:25:42,581 --> 00:25:43,402
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that's the whole point.

378
00:25:43,422 --> 00:25:44,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the possibility is still there.

379
00:25:44,542 --> 00:25:44,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

380
00:25:44,742 --> 00:25:44,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

381
00:25:45,875 --> 00:25:46,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

382
00:25:46,375 --> 00:25:47,416
[SPEAKER_00]: The cake is presented.

383
00:25:47,496 --> 00:25:48,657
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility is there.

384
00:25:49,037 --> 00:25:51,739
[SPEAKER_00]: But your surmounting of the possibility didn't end yesterday.

385
00:25:52,399 --> 00:25:53,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the possibility is not split.

386
00:25:54,080 --> 00:25:55,621
[SPEAKER_00]: It's present and enduring right here and now.

387
00:25:56,001 --> 00:25:58,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was the same possibility that you had in the past.

388
00:25:58,563 --> 00:26:00,884
[SPEAKER_00]: It'll be the same possibility you have in the future.

389
00:26:01,705 --> 00:26:02,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

390
00:26:02,965 --> 00:26:06,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by surmounting the possibility, you have surmounted your past sensuality.

391
00:26:07,548 --> 00:26:10,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're surmounting any future engagement with sensuality.

392
00:26:10,490 --> 00:26:11,711
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the Buddha said.

393
00:26:11,911 --> 00:26:15,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of what object... You're the one who surmounts the sense of self.

394
00:26:16,070 --> 00:26:21,615
[SPEAKER_00]: ...correctly sees it presently, he doesn't think in the past, I was, and he doesn't think in the future, I will be.

395
00:26:21,855 --> 00:26:29,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by seeing it correctly, that possibility of mistake of I am right here, right now, he's already... his past is included in it, and the future is included in it.

396
00:26:29,943 --> 00:26:30,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

397
00:26:30,143 --> 00:26:33,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're all subordinate to the presence of that possibility.

398
00:26:48,984 --> 00:26:50,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind is still not oppressed currently.

399
00:26:52,207 --> 00:26:53,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility of it?

400
00:26:53,828 --> 00:26:56,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility of the mind being oppressed by the unwholesome?

401
00:27:00,073 --> 00:27:11,182
[SPEAKER_00]: You could just even stay with that question for a while as well, because it will clarify the domain of the possibility, without overly particularizing it, so to speak, overly rationalizing it, nor mystifying it either.

402
00:27:12,943 --> 00:27:16,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I liable to being affected by the unwholesome?

403
00:27:18,090 --> 00:27:19,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't rush answering it.

404
00:27:24,254 --> 00:27:33,941
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll see that what you are thinking about, what you are kind of refraining from an answer to an extent, is the domain you asked about, is the domain that you need to understand.

405
00:27:34,362 --> 00:27:41,687
[SPEAKER_00]: And often, the reason why people don't understand it is because they don't see it, because they never ask the question in the right way, as a way of establishing the mind.

406
00:27:42,487 --> 00:27:43,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's how even jhānas are established.

407
00:27:43,988 --> 00:27:46,110
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Maha Moggallāna, when the Buddha encouraged him,

408
00:27:47,267 --> 00:27:53,112
[SPEAKER_00]: To practice steady his mind in jhānas, because it would be much to his fruit and benefit, he goes under the root of the tree and says, first jhāna, first jhāna.

409
00:27:53,132 --> 00:27:54,113
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the first jhāna?

410
00:27:55,014 --> 00:27:56,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Buddhist scribe, he said, it's this.

411
00:27:56,915 --> 00:27:57,896
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the presence of this.

412
00:27:57,976 --> 00:28:01,219
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the presence of this factor, or that factor, whatever you translate it.

413
00:28:01,819 --> 00:28:04,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he was understanding it, understanding it, he answered it.

414
00:28:04,441 --> 00:28:05,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, can I do more of this?

415
00:28:05,642 --> 00:28:07,304
[SPEAKER_00]: As in further development of the same principle?

416
00:28:07,324 --> 00:28:07,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

417
00:28:07,664 --> 00:28:08,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Second, third, fourth.

418
00:28:17,676 --> 00:28:22,357
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by asking the question, am I liable to the pressure of the unwholesome?

419
00:28:22,437 --> 00:28:24,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I liable to the pressure of centrality?

420
00:28:25,038 --> 00:28:26,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I liable to centrality?

421
00:28:26,158 --> 00:28:27,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I liable to your will?

422
00:28:27,958 --> 00:28:35,420
[SPEAKER_00]: And not like, yes, no, well, this means that, and over-rationalizing it, or providing psychologizing answers.

423
00:28:36,420 --> 00:28:41,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Just by staying with that question for a while, you're staying with the domain that you directed your question to.

424
00:28:41,141 --> 00:28:42,662
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you start to discern the domain.

425
00:28:42,942 --> 00:28:47,583
[SPEAKER_00]: By not jumping to give an answer, but instead, discerning the answer.

426
00:28:49,748 --> 00:28:52,091
[SPEAKER_00]: on account of the enduring domain that you asked about.

427
00:28:56,737 --> 00:29:04,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could just... somebody can just spend a day having the mind established upon this question of being liable to unwholesome.

428
00:29:05,638 --> 00:29:09,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Just spend your day with that being an implication of your day, whatever you do.

429
00:29:10,660 --> 00:29:12,981
[SPEAKER_00]: That unwholesome, you might be liable to it.

430
00:29:13,701 --> 00:29:23,244
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll see, when a particular unwholesome arises, you realize, yes, this could have only arisen on the overall liability towards the possibility of unwholesome.

431
00:29:24,304 --> 00:29:27,706
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not that that possibility has become this particular one.

432
00:29:27,786 --> 00:29:29,106
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they're two separate domains.

433
00:29:29,986 --> 00:29:33,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the possibility of my liability to unwholesome states throughout the day,

434
00:29:34,572 --> 00:29:35,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get an unwholesome state.

435
00:29:36,133 --> 00:29:37,833
[SPEAKER_00]: There is still a possibility of being liable.

436
00:29:38,093 --> 00:29:40,134
[SPEAKER_00]: That's now also verified on the present level.

437
00:29:41,094 --> 00:29:46,696
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't... you stop caring about the present level in that direct manner, because you realize that's not where the problem is.

438
00:29:46,736 --> 00:29:50,298
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is on the level of the mind and being affected by the domain of it.

439
00:29:50,758 --> 00:29:56,840
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of possibility of sensuality, of will, and so on.

440
00:29:56,940 --> 00:30:04,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, if you become mindful of the... of your liability towards unwholesome,

441
00:30:05,072 --> 00:30:11,755
[SPEAKER_00]: That in itself, that mindfulness becomes a protection against unwholesome, actually affecting you.

442
00:30:12,516 --> 00:30:22,961
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not be completely out of it, but mindfulness of the possibility of unwholesome affecting you, means no particular unwholesome can affect you.

443
00:30:23,021 --> 00:30:28,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the fundamental liability to dukkha, means no particular dukkha can affect you.

444
00:30:32,285 --> 00:30:38,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not about removing that... you can't remove that possibility, because it's not yours to begin with.

445
00:30:38,148 --> 00:30:47,773
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, all you have to do is develop mindfulness in regard to it, to the extent necessary, whereby you stop entertaining slightest notion that this is pleasurable and for me.

446
00:30:49,054 --> 00:30:49,914
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

447
00:30:50,374 --> 00:30:51,275
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's often the problem.

448
00:30:51,315 --> 00:30:52,475
[SPEAKER_00]: People overshoot the mark.

449
00:30:52,896 --> 00:30:55,617
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see where the problem is, then they overdo it and they end up denying things.

450
00:30:55,977 --> 00:30:59,359
[SPEAKER_00]: I must stop thinking, I must stop looking, I must... blind, deaf man becomes the goal.

451
00:31:00,334 --> 00:31:02,496
[SPEAKER_01]: I must not do this, I must not do this, I must not do this.

452
00:31:03,637 --> 00:31:05,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't know what to do, you start denying everything.

453
00:31:07,741 --> 00:31:13,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you must not engage with sensuality or with ill-will on a particular basis, means the body needs to be restrained, speech needs to be restrained.

454
00:31:17,251 --> 00:31:24,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind needs to be restrained and the restraint of mind is through the restraint of understanding the possibility of being liable to it in the first place.

455
00:31:26,035 --> 00:31:32,917
[SPEAKER_00]: By you blaming the possibility and trying to get rid of it, means you not seeing where the problem is.

456
00:31:34,178 --> 00:31:38,179
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person, instead of, like, all this dhamma, stop thinking about it, is impossible.

457
00:31:38,259 --> 00:31:40,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I is thinking about sights.

458
00:31:41,500 --> 00:31:43,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Animals want to feed, and that's what we say.

459
00:31:43,101 --> 00:31:48,143
[SPEAKER_00]: You tie them in, they'll be thinking about their feeding grounds, where they used to go and eat and do stuff.

460
00:31:48,843 --> 00:31:49,863
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not your problem.

461
00:31:50,204 --> 00:31:53,025
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is, why am I emotionally bothered by this, literally?

462
00:31:53,705 --> 00:31:53,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

463
00:31:54,945 --> 00:31:57,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure of sensuality will arise again in the future.

464
00:31:58,728 --> 00:32:03,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Why, or will I, be emotionally affected by that?

465
00:32:04,033 --> 00:32:05,134
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is yes, why?

466
00:32:05,394 --> 00:32:06,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I emotionally affected by this?

467
00:32:07,315 --> 00:32:16,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize, people would realize that they would be emotionally affected by this, because they never dwelt long enough on the fact that you can't be emotionally affected by it, even if you want to.

468
00:32:16,523 --> 00:32:18,425
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you can't have it be yours.

469
00:32:20,263 --> 00:32:21,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you want to.

470
00:32:21,624 --> 00:32:22,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it isn't.

471
00:32:22,964 --> 00:32:23,785
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't created it.

472
00:32:23,885 --> 00:32:24,805
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not bound with you.

473
00:32:25,986 --> 00:32:29,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Its own saṅkharas are its respective senses, inseparable from.

474
00:32:31,248 --> 00:32:36,250
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you need to do is then, oh, all you need to do is stop regarding it wrongly and then it will stop affecting you.

475
00:32:37,591 --> 00:32:40,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And you stop regarding it wrongly if you start seeing it, no, this is dangerous.

476
00:32:45,448 --> 00:32:50,111
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how you arrive at that, regarding it as a danger, it's up to a person himself.

477
00:32:50,571 --> 00:32:58,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether through simple faith in the Buddha as being ultimately right, knowing what he's talking about, and you already have verified a great deal of it, so you just have faith.

478
00:32:59,196 --> 00:33:03,498
[SPEAKER_00]: What he said himself, it's worth, regarding it as dangerous, it's going to have tremendous benefit.

479
00:33:03,858 --> 00:33:05,499
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't quite know how, but I'll do it.

480
00:33:05,939 --> 00:33:08,080
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, that's enough to do it.

481
00:33:08,260 --> 00:33:10,902
[SPEAKER_00]: That basis of faith, which will then result in knowledge.

482
00:33:11,769 --> 00:33:15,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if you need more of, like, vipassana and self-questioning, hammering it further, fine.

483
00:33:15,911 --> 00:33:18,371
[SPEAKER_00]: You're gonna arrive at the same conclusions, because that's what the Buddha did say.

484
00:33:19,692 --> 00:33:21,813
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't fabricate, like, a false advertisement.

485
00:33:21,973 --> 00:33:22,113
No.

486
00:33:32,937 --> 00:33:39,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the possibility of sensuality affects a person presently, because he's still

487
00:33:45,755 --> 00:33:47,556
[SPEAKER_00]: He hasn't seen pleasure of it.

488
00:33:48,117 --> 00:33:56,902
[SPEAKER_00]: He might have seen how various sensual objects are ugly, repulsive, dissatisfactory, dangerous.

489
00:33:57,543 --> 00:34:01,345
[SPEAKER_00]: But he hasn't seen that pleasure in regard to them is dangerous.

490
00:34:02,026 --> 00:34:02,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

491
00:34:02,266 --> 00:34:04,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he kept looking on the level of the objects.

492
00:34:06,368 --> 00:34:10,051
[SPEAKER_00]: But pleasure is neither in the sight nor in the eye.

493
00:34:11,250 --> 00:34:12,471
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in regard to the two.

494
00:34:15,474 --> 00:34:22,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure of the touch is neither in the that which is touching, the object of the touch, nor your body.

495
00:34:23,120 --> 00:34:24,301
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in regard to the two.

496
00:34:26,343 --> 00:34:31,447
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it needs to be seen for what it is.

497
00:34:33,388 --> 00:34:34,069
[SPEAKER_00]: And that pleasure...

498
00:34:34,089 --> 00:34:34,730
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's not enough.

499
00:34:34,810 --> 00:34:35,971
[SPEAKER_00]: This pleasure is not... No, it's real.

500
00:34:36,111 --> 00:34:36,771
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a real pleasure.

501
00:34:36,791 --> 00:34:38,613
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you understand the gratification of it.

502
00:34:39,523 --> 00:34:42,324
[SPEAKER_00]: But you understand that that pleasure is dangerous.

503
00:34:43,765 --> 00:34:45,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you rewrite the significance.

504
00:34:45,745 --> 00:34:50,687
[SPEAKER_00]: You rewrite the signs and tokens of central pleasure, which is factual.

505
00:34:52,008 --> 00:35:01,351
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead of dear, agreeable, welcoming, non-threatening, it's seen as threatening, murderous, dangerous, perilous.

506
00:35:02,212 --> 00:35:02,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Same pleasure.

507
00:35:04,473 --> 00:35:07,834
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to rewrite those signs and tokens, you need to see it for what it is.

508
00:35:08,963 --> 00:35:11,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you won't be applying it to the right level.

509
00:35:19,692 --> 00:35:30,041
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, when the question, when a person, if a person presents a question, so, okay, currently I'm not bothered by any sensuality or real world, but can I be bothered?

510
00:35:30,081 --> 00:35:30,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be bothered?

511
00:35:31,542 --> 00:35:32,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, maybe, probably.

512
00:35:33,484 --> 00:35:35,185
[SPEAKER_00]: That is a presently recent experience.

513
00:35:36,466 --> 00:35:37,347
[SPEAKER_00]: That probability,

514
00:35:38,204 --> 00:35:42,706
[SPEAKER_00]: And instead of jumping at answer, stay mindful of that peripheral probability.

515
00:35:44,047 --> 00:35:45,387
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the domain I'm talking about.

516
00:35:47,108 --> 00:35:56,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so now I'll go talk, eat, walk, sit, lay down, while probability of being affected by unwholesome still remains.

517
00:35:58,613 --> 00:36:00,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you might want to be keen to get rid of it.

518
00:36:00,874 --> 00:36:03,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you might be keen to solve it.

519
00:36:04,928 --> 00:36:06,969
[SPEAKER_00]: To figure it out, to free yourself from it.

520
00:36:07,369 --> 00:36:07,929
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's good.

521
00:36:08,529 --> 00:36:12,210
[SPEAKER_00]: But what it's not good is when that becomes craving for getting rid of it.

522
00:36:13,231 --> 00:36:14,751
[SPEAKER_00]: That means, ah, you're bound by it again.

523
00:36:16,692 --> 00:36:23,434
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to allow it to exist to its own extent, the possibility of sensuality, not actual engagement with sensuality.

524
00:36:25,134 --> 00:36:28,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And then stop denying, stop trying to get rid of it, because that's not on you either.

525
00:36:29,115 --> 00:36:32,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like blaming the things that your dog wants to eat.

526
00:36:32,617 --> 00:36:33,037
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

527
00:36:33,197 --> 00:36:33,857
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ridiculous.

528
00:36:35,100 --> 00:36:36,521
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the sense wants to eat.

529
00:36:36,661 --> 00:36:37,902
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what animals feeds on.

530
00:36:38,002 --> 00:36:39,263
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just how it works.

531
00:36:42,805 --> 00:36:48,568
[SPEAKER_00]: By you deriving emotional pleasure from engaging with that type of food on account of I, that's on you.

532
00:36:50,189 --> 00:36:56,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Or emotional dissatisfaction on account of lack of the food for your I, that's on you.

533
00:36:57,578 --> 00:37:03,481
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you develop emotional indifference in regard to the pleasure, in regard to the whole sense domain.

534
00:37:03,922 --> 00:37:05,423
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you free yourself from the sense domain.

535
00:37:05,703 --> 00:37:06,503
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we spoke before.

536
00:37:06,843 --> 00:37:13,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop wanting the wanting of sensual pleasure by your senses.

537
00:37:13,127 --> 00:37:18,490
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't stop wanting of your senses directly, because they're not yours to begin with.

538
00:37:20,612 --> 00:37:23,653
[SPEAKER_00]: You restrain them, sure, to create a basis for the work.

539
00:37:24,434 --> 00:37:26,315
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you stop wanting their wanting,

540
00:37:27,568 --> 00:37:31,631
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you sever your liability to anything unwholesome, pretty much.

541
00:37:36,956 --> 00:37:44,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, that possibility of liability, for somebody who has developed his mind in regard to it,

542
00:37:44,848 --> 00:37:49,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it still stays the possibility of sensuality, but the liability is gone.

543
00:37:49,991 --> 00:37:51,813
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you are not li... you went beyond it.

544
00:37:53,093 --> 00:37:55,435
[SPEAKER_00]: The domain of sensuality with possibility remains.

545
00:37:55,935 --> 00:37:57,516
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why arahant can still discern it.

546
00:37:58,057 --> 00:37:59,057
[SPEAKER_00]: But he went beyond it.

547
00:37:59,538 --> 00:38:06,422
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't dismantle the structure of I and the sights, as people would sometimes try to do, which is like a wrong view of a shortcut.

548
00:38:07,503 --> 00:38:08,483
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he went beyond it.

549
00:38:08,524 --> 00:38:10,065
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the possibility of the body.

550
00:38:10,265 --> 00:38:10,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

551
00:38:10,685 --> 00:38:11,205
[SPEAKER_01]: In the world.

552
00:38:11,646 --> 00:38:13,767
[SPEAKER_00]: But he's not liable to engage with it anymore.

553
00:38:15,206 --> 00:38:17,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the beautiful sights still remain.

554
00:38:17,608 --> 00:38:18,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Sound, smell, taste, touches.

555
00:38:19,670 --> 00:38:21,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Agreeable, disagreeable, comfort, discomfort.

556
00:38:21,512 --> 00:38:22,733
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that's central anymore.

557
00:38:22,753 --> 00:38:23,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he sees the danger.

558
00:38:24,455 --> 00:38:25,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he's seen the danger.

559
00:38:25,917 --> 00:38:28,019
[SPEAKER_01]: He's seen the danger in the pleasure.

560
00:38:28,099 --> 00:38:33,944
[SPEAKER_00]: And he's seen the danger in the pleasure to the extent necessary for that liability to be completely rewritten.

561
00:38:34,838 --> 00:38:37,118
[SPEAKER_00]: On the level of signs and tokens and nimittas of it.

562
00:38:37,659 --> 00:38:43,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, when sensuality arises, there are no nimittas of subha and agreeable, beautiful.

563
00:38:43,700 --> 00:38:47,761
[SPEAKER_00]: There are nimittas of this has been despised and abandoned and dangerous and disgusting.

564
00:38:48,581 --> 00:38:49,781
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means now.

565
00:38:51,141 --> 00:38:53,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is general danger, disgust in it.

566
00:38:54,462 --> 00:38:58,463
[SPEAKER_00]: By you being gratuitously bound to the enjoying pleasure of it, you don't see it.

567
00:39:05,642 --> 00:39:14,447
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm saying this because whoever would try to practice this, inevitably, the mind would then try to get rid of this possibility, as in, that would be the way of fixing the problem.

568
00:39:14,507 --> 00:39:15,008
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

569
00:39:15,848 --> 00:39:19,130
[SPEAKER_00]: The way of fixing the problem is developing your mind in regard to that possibility.

570
00:39:19,150 --> 00:39:26,254
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, sometimes it's good to not even try and answer the question, just formulate it clearly, clearly, and let it endure.

571
00:39:28,676 --> 00:39:29,536
[SPEAKER_00]: First jhāna, first jhāna.

572
00:39:29,556 --> 00:39:30,577
[SPEAKER_00]: What is first jhāna?

573
00:39:35,376 --> 00:39:36,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Domain of sensuality.

574
00:39:36,497 --> 00:39:37,857
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the domain of sensuality?

575
00:39:38,878 --> 00:39:40,259
[SPEAKER_00]: The liability to sensuality.

576
00:39:40,699 --> 00:39:41,820
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my liability?

577
00:39:44,901 --> 00:39:45,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Let it endure.

578
00:39:47,122 --> 00:39:53,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there will be a phenomenon that you might want to kind of overly define, but that's not the point.

579
00:39:55,527 --> 00:39:58,969
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to rewrite the emotional significance of it, so to speak.

580
00:40:00,089 --> 00:40:01,510
[SPEAKER_00]: So, liability of sensuality.

581
00:40:02,331 --> 00:40:02,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

582
00:40:04,657 --> 00:40:06,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm welcoming it.

583
00:40:07,398 --> 00:40:11,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Because fundamentally, I do not see it as the most perilous thing.

584
00:40:13,521 --> 00:40:22,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, if you don't even have a notion, not even a peripheral degree of sort of recognition of the liability to sensuality, how can you rewrite anything about it?

585
00:40:22,765 --> 00:40:24,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you fully encapsulate it within it.

586
00:40:26,586 --> 00:40:33,189
[SPEAKER_00]: That's even if somebody lives their life perfectly restrained for the rest of their life, still remain bound with sensuality, because they haven't understood that domain, that liability.

587
00:40:39,265 --> 00:40:43,447
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't want to be rewriting the liability, as in the content of your liability.

588
00:40:44,287 --> 00:40:47,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be rewriting, basically, your attitude towards the liability.

589
00:40:48,128 --> 00:40:49,809
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you rewrite its significance.

590
00:40:52,150 --> 00:41:01,393
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could say, someone who practices rightly is liable to suffering, but then he ceases to be liable to that liability of suffering.

591
00:41:01,413 --> 00:41:03,414
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, the body is liable to suffering.

592
00:41:04,034 --> 00:41:05,715
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that, he is liable to.

593
00:41:07,041 --> 00:41:12,144
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Arahant says, any disturbance that's left, it's on account of this body, these five aggregates, until they break apart.

594
00:41:13,465 --> 00:41:13,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Disturbance.

595
00:41:14,106 --> 00:41:16,748
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the most the suffering can wanna be for an Arahant.

596
00:41:17,408 --> 00:41:17,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Disturbance.

597
00:41:18,048 --> 00:41:18,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Not suffering, really.

598
00:41:24,393 --> 00:41:27,635
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the mind is developed in regard to jhānas, then even that disturbance won't be perceived.

599
00:41:35,695 --> 00:41:44,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, clarifying the enduring liability to suffering or sensuality of anything wholesome is not for the sake of getting rid of it, it's for the sake of clarity of it.

600
00:41:46,183 --> 00:41:47,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Having it endure.

601
00:41:47,484 --> 00:41:54,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as long as you're mindful of your liability to sensuality, you cannot possibly engage with sensuality on a particular level.

602
00:41:55,827 --> 00:42:02,788
[SPEAKER_00]: The necessary basis for particular engagement with sensuality is oversight of the liability to sensuality in the first place.

603
00:42:03,369 --> 00:42:04,129
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it would have meant.

604
00:42:04,149 --> 00:42:09,910
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible to engage with sensuality without having already been bound by sensual perceptions.

605
00:42:09,930 --> 00:42:16,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, discerning the liability of sensuality is already perceiving sensuality on that level of possibility.

606
00:42:17,451 --> 00:42:19,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are already sensual perceptions.

607
00:42:23,493 --> 00:42:24,533
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're mindful of them,

608
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:30,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome them, don't entertain them, for as long as they endure, that's all you need to do.

609
00:42:31,805 --> 00:42:32,466
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a gate.

610
00:42:33,266 --> 00:42:33,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Gatekeeper.

611
00:42:34,427 --> 00:42:41,093
[SPEAKER_00]: You then realize, I don't need to go and worry about the particular manifestation of it, because it won't be a problem, because I'm not abandoning this gatepost.

612
00:42:43,555 --> 00:42:47,839
[SPEAKER_00]: My mindfulness, peripheral awareness of the liability to unwholesome.

